# Shortest Path Pattern

Shortest Path problems ask us to find the minimum cost required to travel from a source state to a destination state.

The cost may represent:

- Number of edges
- Number of moves
- Total distance
- Travel time
- Ticket price
- Number of transformations
- Obstacles removed
- Maximum effort
- Probability
- Direction changes

The most important part is not memorizing every algorithm. It is identifying the graph type and selecting the correct algorithm.

---

# 1. Shortest Path Decision Tree

```text
Are all edges equal in cost?
│
├── Yes
│   ├── One source         → BFS
│   └── Multiple sources   → Multi-Source BFS
│
└── No
    │
    ├── Weights are only 0 and 1
    │   └── 0-1 BFS
    │
    ├── All weights are non-negative
    │   └── Dijkstra
    │
    ├── Negative weights are possible
    │   └── Bellman-Ford
    │
    ├── Graph is a DAG
    │   └── Topological Sort + Relaxation
    │
    └── Shortest path between every pair
        └── Floyd-Warshall
```

## Quick Memory Rule

```text
Equal edge cost             → Queue
Multiple starting points    → Multi-Source Queue
Edge weights only 0 and 1   → Deque
Different positive weights  → PriorityQueue
Negative weights            → Bellman-Ford
Weighted DAG                → Topological Sort
All-pairs shortest path     → Floyd-Warshall
```

