# 21. Final Revision Cheat Sheet

```text
1. Identify the state.
2. Identify the possible transitions.
3. Determine the cost of each transition.
4. Check whether there is one source or multiple sources.
5. Check whether an additional condition belongs in the state.
6. Select the shortest-path algorithm.
7. Create the correct distance structure.
8. Perform relaxation whenever a better route is found.
9. Use a parent array if the actual path is required.
```

## Final Algorithm Selection

```text
Unweighted graph
→ BFS

Equal-cost grid movements
→ BFS

Multiple starting points
→ Multi-Source BFS

Edges weighted 0 or 1
→ 0-1 BFS

Different non-negative weights
→ Dijkstra

Negative edge weights
→ Bellman-Ford

Weighted DAG
→ Topological Sort + Relaxation

Every pair of nodes
→ Floyd-Warshall
```

## One-Line Memory Trick

```text
Queue         → Equal cost
Deque         → Cost 0 or 1
PriorityQueue → Different non-negative costs
Relax edges   → Negative weights
Matrix DP     → Every pair of nodes
```

## Core Shortest Path Formula

```java
if (distance[currentNode] + edgeWeight
        < distance[neighbour]) {

    distance[neighbour] =
        distance[currentNode] + edgeWeight;
}
```

This is called relaxation.

It means:

> If reaching the neighbour through the current node is cheaper, update its shortest known distance.
